# Get started with Consul Agentic Runtime Fabric

This page describes how to route your first model request through the [AI Inference Gateway](/consul/docs/agentic-mesh/inference-gateway). You register one model, deploy one gateway, and send a request from a test client in the service mesh. Then you confirm that the gateway routed the request and that it rejects a request it cannot route.

@include '../../../global/partials/alerts/beta.mdx'

@include 'alerts/enterprise-alert.mdx'

## Overview

You complete the following steps:

1. Deploy a model service and register it with Consul.
1. Define an inference pool and deploy an AI Inference Gateway.
1. Deploy a test client.
1. Authorize the client to call the gateway and the gateway to call the model.
1. Send a request and confirm the result.

This page uses the following names. Replace them to match your environment.

| Object | Name |
| --- | --- |
| Model service | `demo-model` |
| `InferencePoolConfig` | `demo-pool` |
| `InferenceGateway` and its Consul service | `demo-inference` |
| Test client | `demo-client` |
| Kubernetes namespace | `default` |

## Requirements

- Consul Agentic Runtime Fabric installed with `ai.enabled` and `ai.inferenceGateway.enabled` set to `true`. Refer to [Install Consul Agentic Runtime Fabric](/consul/docs/agentic-mesh/install).
- `kubectl` access to the cluster.
- A model server that exposes an OpenAI-compatible API, or permission to run one in the cluster.

<!-- TODO: Install page TODO blocks this page. Confirm with the product owner which container registry and image names customers use for the AI Inference Gateway image. Readers cannot complete installation without it. -->

## Deploy and register a model service

Deploy a model server in the mesh and annotate its pod template so that Consul registers it as a model upstream. The following example runs a self-hosted model server that serves an OpenAI-compatible API under `/v1`.

<!-- TODO: Confirm that a self-hosted Ollama server works as a model backend in Consul 2.1, or replace it with a server the product team tests. Ollama serves an OpenAI-compatible API under /v1 on port 11434, which matches the openai protocol and /v1 path defaults. Confirm the image tag, the model name, the resource requests, and how the model gets pulled into the pod. -->

<CodeBlockConfig filename="demo-model.yaml" highlight="31-37">

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: demo-model
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: demo-model
spec:
  selector:
    app: demo-model
  ports:
    - port: 11434
      targetPort: 11434
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: demo-model
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: demo-model
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: demo-model
      annotations:
        consul.hashicorp.com/connect-inject: "true"
        consul.hashicorp.com/ai-role: inference-model
        consul.hashicorp.com/ai-inference-model-protocol: openai
        consul.hashicorp.com/ai-inference-model-path: /v1
        consul.hashicorp.com/service-meta-capabilities: "chat"
        consul.hashicorp.com/service-meta-priority_chat: "0"
        consul.hashicorp.com/service-meta-model_family: "<model-name>"
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: demo-model
      containers:
        - name: demo-model
          image: "<model-server-image>"
          ports:
            - containerPort: 11434
```

</CodeBlockConfig>

The annotations register the service with the `inference-model` role, declare that it serves the `chat` capability as the primary tier, and name the concrete model. The gateway applies the `model_family` value to any request whose model is `auto`. For every model annotation, refer to [Register model services](/consul/docs/agentic-mesh/inference-gateway/configure#register-model-services).

Apply the manifest:

```shell-session
$ kubectl apply --filename demo-model.yaml
```

<!-- TODO: Add a command that confirms Consul registered demo-model with the inference-model role, and show its expected output. Candidates: consul catalog services, or the Consul UI. Confirm which shows the AI role. -->

## Deploy the AI Inference Gateway

Create an inference pool and a gateway that references it. This minimal pool sets the processor to fail closed and does not configure failover, PII protection, or telemetry.

<CodeBlockConfig filename="demo-gateway.yaml">

```yaml
apiVersion: consul.hashicorp.com/v1alpha1
kind: InferencePoolConfig
metadata:
  name: demo-pool
  namespace: default
spec:
  enabled: true
  parentRefs:
    - kind: InferenceGateway
      name: demo-inference
  processor:
    failureMode: closed
---
apiVersion: consul.hashicorp.com/v1alpha1
kind: InferenceGateway
metadata:
  name: demo-inference
  namespace: default
spec:
  poolRef:
    name: demo-pool
```

</CodeBlockConfig>

Apply the manifest:

```shell-session
$ kubectl apply --filename demo-gateway.yaml
```

The controller creates a Deployment, a Service, and an `inference-gateway` configuration entry. Consul registers the gateway under the `InferenceGateway` name, `demo-inference`, on port `8443` by default.

<!-- TODO: Add a command that confirms the gateway is ready, with expected output. Candidates: kubectl get inferencegateway demo-inference, and kubectl rollout status deployment for the gateway. Confirm the Deployment name the controller creates and which status condition means ready. The controller sets a PoolResolved reason, per inferencegateway_controller.go line 50. -->

## Deploy a test client

Deploy a client in the mesh that declares the gateway as an upstream. The client reaches the gateway on a local port, `8080` in this example.

<!-- TODO: Verify that the connect-service-upstreams annotation is the supported way for a workload to reach an AI Inference Gateway. The configure page says agents send requests "over their mesh upstream". Confirm the annotation format for this gateway kind. -->

<CodeBlockConfig filename="demo-client.yaml" highlight="30-31">

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: demo-client
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: demo-client
spec:
  selector:
    app: demo-client
  ports:
    - port: 80
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: demo-client
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: demo-client
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: demo-client
      annotations:
        consul.hashicorp.com/connect-inject: "true"
        consul.hashicorp.com/connect-service-upstreams: "demo-inference:8080"
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: demo-client
      containers:
        - name: demo-client
          image: curlimages/curl:latest
          command: ["sleep", "infinity"]
```

</CodeBlockConfig>

<!-- TODO: Pin the curl image to a specific tag before publishing. Fix the highlight line numbers if the manifest changes. -->

Apply the manifest:

```shell-session
$ kubectl apply --filename demo-client.yaml
```

## Authorize traffic

Service intentions decide which workloads can call the gateway and which models the gateway can reach. Allow the client to call the gateway and the gateway to call the model.

<CodeBlockConfig filename="demo-intentions.yaml">

```yaml
apiVersion: consul.hashicorp.com/v1alpha1
kind: ServiceIntentions
metadata:
  name: demo-inference
spec:
  destination:
    name: demo-inference
  sources:
    - name: demo-client
      action: allow
---
apiVersion: consul.hashicorp.com/v1alpha1
kind: ServiceIntentions
metadata:
  name: demo-model
spec:
  destination:
    name: demo-model
  sources:
    - name: demo-inference
      action: allow
```

</CodeBlockConfig>

Apply the manifest:

```shell-session
$ kubectl apply --filename demo-intentions.yaml
```

For more information, refer to [Service intentions](/consul/docs/reference/config-entry/service-intentions).

## Send a request

Send an OpenAI-compatible chat request from the client. The `x-inference-specialization` header names the capability, and the gateway routes the request to the highest-priority model that serves it. The `auto` model tells the gateway to use the model's `model_family` value.

<!-- TODO: Confirm the request path a client sends to the gateway. The processor lives in a separate codebase (consul-ai-apps), not in consul-enterprise or consul-k8s. Envoy matches path prefix / on the gateway, and the model is registered with base path /v1, so /v1/chat/completions is the expected path. Confirm whether the client sends /v1/chat/completions or /chat/completions. -->

```shell-session
$ kubectl exec deploy/demo-client --container demo-client -- \
    curl --silent --show-error http://localhost:8080/v1/chat/completions \
    --header "Content-Type: application/json" \
    --header "x-inference-specialization: chat" \
    --data '{"model": "auto", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Say hello in one sentence."}]}'
```

The gateway returns the model's response in the OpenAI chat completion format.

<!-- TODO: Paste real output from a test run here, trimmed to the fields a reader needs, such as choices[0].message.content and model. Confirm whether the response or its headers identify the model that served the request. -->

## Confirm that the gateway enforces routing

Send the same request without the capability header. The gateway has no route for it and fails closed.

```shell-session
$ kubectl exec deploy/demo-client --container demo-client -- \
    curl --silent --output /dev/null --write-out "%{http_code}\n" \
    http://localhost:8080/v1/chat/completions \
    --header "Content-Type: application/json" \
    --data '{"model": "auto", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Say hello in one sentence."}]}'
```

```plaintext hideClipboard
503
```

A request that names a capability no registered model serves also returns `503`.

<!-- TODO: Optionally add a metrics check. The gateway serves Prometheus metrics on port 9090 at /metrics, per inferencegateway_controller.go lines 57 to 59. Metric names come from the processor codebase, consul-ai-apps. Add a port-forward command and name one request counter to look for. -->

## Clean up

Delete the resources you created:

```shell-session
$ kubectl delete --filename demo-intentions.yaml --filename demo-client.yaml \
    --filename demo-gateway.yaml --filename demo-model.yaml
```

## Next steps

- [Configure the AI Inference Gateway](/consul/docs/agentic-mesh/inference-gateway/configure) to add failover tiers, PII protection, and telemetry.
- [Configure the MCP Sidecar](/consul/docs/agentic-mesh/mcp-sidecar/configure) to govern agent tool calls.
- [Configure auto-instrumentation](/consul/docs/agentic-mesh/observability/configure-auto-instrumentation) to trace model and tool calls.
- [Configure cycle detection](/consul/docs/agentic-mesh/cycle-detection/configure) to block agents stuck in loops.
